# Project Summary
Problem: Forklifts, loaders, and dump trucks have blind spots that put nearby workers at risk of being struck. Distance-only alarms can generate unnecessary warnings because they ignore where a worker is relative to the vehicle.
Approach: Halo explores using a worker’s position relative to vehicle blind spots and configurable danger zones to provide more relevant warnings to workers and operators. We tested Wi-Fi Fine Timing Measurement (FTM) with multiple ESP32 devices and used nonlinear trilateration to estimate worker position in 2D.
Results: At natHacks 2026, we built a working web prototype with an interactive blind-spot simulator, alert visualization, event history, vehicle records, incident investigation, and corrective action tracking. We also built a live 2D positioning view fed by cloud-uploaded range measurements. Ranging inaccuracies prevented reliable real-world positioning and full hardware-based hazard detection. The prototype demonstrates how safety teams can review warnings, investigate potential near misses, and assign corrective actions.


What inspired us
What inspired us: A worker can be only a few steps from a forklift or loader and still be outside the operator’s view. Two workers at the same distance from a vehicle can face different risks depending on where they’re standing and how the vehicle is moving. That led us to explore whether vehicle-specific blind zones could make proximity warnings more relevant. We also wanted to help safety teams investigate warnings and act on recurring hazards.
How we built it
How we built it: we built a browser-based simulator that lets users configure blind zones, set worker and vehicle routes, and observe warnings. We also built a safety dashboard where managers can review alerts, link incidents to vehicles, classify events, assign corrective actions, and track cases through closure. Supabase stores organization records.
For the hardware experiment, we collected Wi-Fi Fine Timing Measurement (FTM) ranges from three ESP32 anchors. We uploaded those measurements to the cloud and used nonlinear least-squares trilateration to estimate and display the worker’s position on a live 2D map.
What we learned and what challenged us
Main challenges: Our main challenge was getting consistent distance readings. Environmental interference and fluctuating measurements made the estimated position too unreliable for hardware-based hazard detection. We learned that a positioning algorithm depends on the quality of the measurements it receives, especially when a small error could put a worker on the wrong side of a danger-zone boundary.
Keeping experimental telemetry separate from simulated scenarios and sample events let us demonstrate the software workflow independently. The demo shows how a safety manager can go from reviewing a warning to assigning a corrective action. The next step is to improve ranging accuracy and test physical warnings for workers and operators under controlled conditions.
